-- Rollback 0050 — drop the reach column + enum, AFTER neutralizing
-- every durable artifact only ADR-0028 code can handle safely.
--
-- WHY THE NEUTRALIZATION EXISTS (Codex stop-review round 3): the
-- pre-0050 build has no partition logic — its delete-undo applies a
-- uniform +INBOX to whatever ids a reverse job carries. Any all-mail
-- artifact left live (an unexpired undo token whose split matters, a
-- queued reverse of an all-mail delete, a frozen wide forward set)
-- becomes an inbox flood the moment the old build touches it. So this
-- script expires / disarms those artifacts first; the trade is honest
-- and bounded: those specific undos become unavailable (Gmail Trash's
-- own 30-day recovery still applies) instead of destructive.
--
-- ORDER OF OPERATIONS:
--   1. Stop the API and every worker (do NOT deploy the old build yet).
--   2. Drain the action queue in Redis (e.g. obliterate the label-action
--      queue) — a BullMQ job replayed by the old build bypasses row
--      state and would mutate with the old semantics.
--   3. Run this script.
--   4. Deploy the pre-0050 build and start services.
--
-- Step A — expire every delete-undo token whose inbox/archive split
-- matters (strict subset ⇒ archived ids are present). Inbox-only
-- deletes (partition = whole set) keep their tokens: the old build's
-- uniform +INBOX is exactly correct for them.
UPDATE "undo_journal"
SET "expires_at" = now()
WHERE "action_kind" = 'delete'
  AND "expires_at" > now()
  AND ("payload" ? 'inboxMessageIds')
  AND jsonb_typeof("payload"->'messageIds') = 'array'
  AND jsonb_array_length("payload"->'inboxMessageIds') < jsonb_array_length("payload"->'messageIds');
--> statement-breakpoint
-- Step B — TERMINALIZE in-flight REVERSE jobs of those same deletes:
-- clear their id sets and fail them with a greppable code. Their
-- tokens were expired above, so nothing can re-enqueue them. The
-- 'failed' state is the truthful terminal — the build able to execute
-- the recorded split no longer exists. Residual if the queue-drain
-- step is skipped: the old build does not honor 'failed' as terminal,
-- so a replayed BullMQ job would reprocess the row — with the id set
-- empty it mutates NOTHING, but it would falsely stamp the journal
-- reverted and flip the row done. Drain the queue; the DB state alone
-- keeps the failure non-destructive, not invisible.
UPDATE "action_jobs"
SET "resolved_message_ids" = '{}',
    "status" = 'failed',
    "error_code" = 'ROLLBACK_0050_ALL_MAIL_UNDO_DISARMED',
    "updated_at" = now()
FROM "undo_journal" u
WHERE "action_jobs"."undo_token" = u."token"
  AND "action_jobs"."direction" = 'reverse'
  AND "action_jobs"."verb" = 'delete'
  AND "action_jobs"."status" IN ('queued', 'executing')
  AND (u."payload" ? 'inboxMessageIds')
  AND jsonb_typeof(u."payload"->'messageIds') = 'array'
  AND jsonb_array_length(u."payload"->'inboxMessageIds') < jsonb_array_length(u."payload"->'messageIds');
--> statement-breakpoint
-- Step C — TERMINALIZE in-flight FORWARD all-mail deletes: fail them
-- and clear their frozen id sets. Leaving them queued would have the
-- old build silently execute a NARROWER action than the preview the
-- user confirmed — a D226 preview-contract violation dressed up as a
-- migration detail. Failing is the truthful outcome: the build that
-- could execute the confirmed scope is gone; the user sees the
-- failure and can re-decide. The id sets are cleared as
-- defense-in-depth: if a replayed queue job makes the old build
-- reprocess the row anyway (it does not honor 'failed'), it resolves
-- inbox-only — strictly narrower, never the wide set whose legacy
-- journal would flood on undo. For an 'executing' row whose crashed
-- attempt already trashed part of the wide set, that remainder stays
-- in Gmail Trash unjournaled — recoverable there for ~30 days, and
-- never re-inboxed by surprise.
UPDATE "action_jobs"
SET "resolved_message_ids" = '{}',
    "status" = 'failed',
    "error_code" = 'ROLLBACK_0050_ALL_MAIL_SCOPE_UNAVAILABLE',
    "updated_at" = now()
WHERE "direction" = 'forward'
  AND "verb" = 'delete'
  AND "reach" = 'all_mail'
  AND "status" IN ('queued', 'executing');
--> statement-breakpoint
ALTER TABLE "action_jobs" DROP CONSTRAINT IF EXISTS "action_jobs_reach_verb_check";
--> statement-breakpoint
ALTER TABLE "action_jobs" DROP COLUMN IF EXISTS "reach";
--> statement-breakpoint
DROP TYPE IF EXISTS "action_reach";
--
-- DONE rows need nothing beyond Step A: their payloads are untouched
-- history, and with the flood-capable tokens expired the old build can
-- never be asked to reverse them. Re-applying 0050 later restores full
-- semantics for NEW actions; the expired tokens stay expired (undo
-- availability is not resurrected retroactively).
